\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces The effect survives a change of state space, a change of generative objective, and a $5\times $ range of clutter. The rigid body is an L-shaped union of five spheres whose state is a full pose, chosen to have trivial symmetry so the domain is not secretly easier; both models are below their own floor at $20$ epochs and only the reduced one clears it. The second column is each model's own best-validation checkpoint, epoch $155$ for the world frame and $159$ for the reduction, so neither had stopped improving when the runs ended. The density rows are freshly generated layouts at densities neither model was trained on. Single seed except the DDPM $20$-epoch row.}}{7}{table.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:replications}{{4}{7}{The effect survives a change of state space, a change of generative objective, and a $5\times $ range of clutter. The rigid body is an L-shaped union of five spheres whose state is a full pose, chosen to have trivial symmetry so the domain is not secretly easier; both models are below their own floor at $20$ epochs and only the reduced one clears it. The second column is each model's own best-validation checkpoint, epoch $155$ for the world frame and $159$ for the reduction, so neither had stopped improving when the runs ended. The density rows are freshly generated layouts at densities neither model was trained on. Single seed except the DDPM $20$-epoch row}{table.4}{}}
